  <hasCoreUnits rdf:resource="#HIST3004"/>
  <hasCoreUnits rdf:resource="#HIST3007"/>
  <hasCoreUnits rdf:resource="#HIST3010"/>
  <hasCoreUnits rdf:resource="#HIST3013"/>
  <hasCoreUnits rdf:resource="#HIST3014"/>
  <hasCoreUnits rdf:resource="#HIST3017"/>
  <hasCoreUnits rdf:resource="#HIST3456"/>
  <hasCoreUnits rdf:resource="#HIST3917"/>
  <hasMajorCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MJD-HISTY</hasMajorCode>
  <hasMajorTitle rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">History</hasMajorTitle>
  <hasMajorSchool rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Humanities</hasMajorSchool>
  <hasMajorDescription rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Its scope spans millennia, its object of study is humanity, its sources are everywhere and everything. 
History's endeavour is unashamedly vast, daunting and challenging. In submitting the past to the scrutiny of the present, it explores among so much more the experience of wars, revolutions, conflicts and diasporas, nations, empires, economies, cultures, emotions and people from the most elevated to the lowliest. In History there are no taboo areas and no questions are off limits. Doing History means learning how to learn about the past, it means engaging with its wonders and horrors, and it means joining in its telling and retelling. Doing History immerses you in discovery, debate, discussion, understanding, surprise and awe, and it requires of you rigour, reason, questioning, imagination and passion. By doing History, you become part of the process by which humanity's memory itself comes to be made.</hasMajorDescription>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

