<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#IREL1001">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Unit"/>
  <hasTeachingPeriods rdf:resource="#Semester 1"/>
  <hasUnitLocations rdf:resource="#UWA (Perth)"/>
  <hasUnitModes rdf:resource="#On-campus"/>
  <isInMajors rdf:resource="#MJD-POLSC"/>
  <isInMajors rdf:resource="#major9"/>
  <isInMajors rdf:resource="#major3"/>
  <hasUnitOutcomes rdf:resource="#understand, explain and engage with the key international political issues that relate to climate change."/>
  <hasUnitOutcomes rdf:resource="#identify the types of actors that participate in international climate politics."/>
  <hasUnitOutcomes rdf:resource="#demonstrate a basic understanding of how the interaction between these actors produces environmental outcomes."/>
  <hasUnitOutcomes rdf:resource="#demonstrate a basic understanding of the concepts, theoretical frameworks and approaches required to understand  international politics as it relates to climate change."/>
  <hasUnitOutcomes rdf:resource="#conduct research and analyse evidence from a range of relevant sources, to construct a logical and persuasive argument."/>
  <hasUnitOutcomes rdf:resource="#develop skills to undertake group projects and tasks, to work collaboratively to examine environmental issues."/>
  <hasUnitAssessments rdf:resource="#tutorial activities"/>
  <hasUnitAssessments rdf:resource="#test"/>
  <hasUnitAssessments rdf:resource="#Essay"/>
  <hasContactRequirement rdf:resource="#contactrequirement484"/>
  <hasUnitCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">IREL1001</hasUnitCode>
  <hasUnitTitle rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The International Politics of Climate Change</hasUnitTitle>
  <hasUnitSchool rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Social Sciences</hasUnitSchool>
  <hasUnitLevel rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</hasUnitLevel>
  <hasUnitDescription rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">This unit introduces students to the international politics of climate change. The unit first looks briefly at theories of environmental politics, using concepts from international relations and environmental politics, to introduce students to debates on how we should think about the planet and its politics. It then looks at key actors that participate in climate politics, including states, intergovernmental organisations, transnational corporations and social movements, and how these actors clash to produce political and environmental outcomes. Its case studies include the United Nations process for dealing with climate change, and key environmental regions such as the Himalayan watershed, the Arctic and Antarctic, and the Amazon rainforest. The unit also takes students through key contemporary issues such as borders and frontiers in the contemporary world, and the positive and negative impact of technology on the climate. Students are introduced to the politics of climate change, why it is so hard to deal with, and begin to think through what political solutions to environmental problems might look like.</hasUnitDescription>
  <hasCreditPoints rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</hasCreditPoints>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

